s have eroded in favor of greater social equality, The Son Also Rises proves that movement on the social ladder has changed little over eight centuries. Using a novel technique -- tracking family names over generations to measure social mobility across countries and periods -- renowned economic historian Gregory Clark reveals that mobility rates are lower than conventionally estimated, do not vary across societies, and are resistant to social policies. The good news is that these patterns are driven by strong inheritance of abilities and lineage does not beget unwarranted advantage. The bad news is that much of our fate is predictable from lineage.","thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-the-son-also-rises-surnames-and-the-history-of-social-mobility-gregory-clark.jpg","libraryShelf":{"scheme":"lcc","callNumber":"HT612 .C53 2015","rawCallNumber":"HT612.C53 2015","mainClass":"H","subclass":"HT","completeness":"full_call_number","confidence":"high","matchedBy":"open_library_exact_isbn","sourceId":"source-open-library-lcc-the-son-also-rises-surnames-and-the-history-of-social-mobility-gregory-clark","sourceEditionId":"/books/OL29274504M","sourceWorkId":"/works/OL17550313W","sourceIsbn13":"9780691168371","sort":{"classLetters":"HT","classWholeNumber":612,"cutters":[{"letters":"C","decimalDigits":"53"}],"year":2015}},"readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.046,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0.356,"traits":[{"id":"academic","score":0.38,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Princeton University Press / Princeton University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.28,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Princeton University Press / Princeton University Press"]},{"id":"character_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"family\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement